On Thu, 17 Nov 2005, Nathan wrote:
>
> Doug Freyburger wrote:
>> Doesn't zapping beam and ray wands anger shopkeepers no matter
>> if you hit their items or not?  They don't like there items damaged
>> and aren't interested in aimed-fire vs mis-fire.
>
> I don't know about other wands, but this is not true of
> cancellation. You can cancel your own property anywhere in
> a shop except the doorway or the "free square", and the
> shopkeeper doesn't mind.
>
> By the way, what matters is where the cancelled object is,
> not where you are. If you leave an object in one of the two bad
> spaces and cancel it from down the hall, the shopkeeper still
> calls you a thief and gets angry.

   Your observation is accurate, but not your conclusion. If you zap
and cancel one of your /own/ objects, while both of you are in the
shop, you still get called a thief even when the cancelled object is
on a "free" space (the doorway or the space just inside the doorway).

The following patch to "zap.c" seems to fix the bug. It just checks
to make sure that the cancelled object is both inside the shop /and/
on a non-"free" space, before it makes the shopkeeper care.

 	case OBJ_FLOOR:
 		objroom = *in_rooms(obj->ox, obj->oy, SHOPBASE);
 		shkp = shop_keeper(objroom);
 		if (!shkp || !inhishop(shkp)) return;
  +              if (!costly_spot(obj->ox, obj->oy)) return;
 		if (costly_spot(u.ux, u.uy) && objroom == *u.ushops) {
 		    Norep("You cancel it, you pay for it!");

